From: VyOS LS1046A Build <build@vyos.io>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:36:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: sfp: tolerate checksum-broken OEM SFP-H10G-B-CU1M DAC

The unbranded "OEM" 10G DAC module (vendor_pn "SFP-H10G-B-CU1M", a 1 m
direct-attach copper cable) ships with a base-structure EEPROM checksum
byte that never matches the computed value:

  sfp sfp-xfi0: EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0xf0 != 0xf3
  sfp sfp-xfi1: EEPROM base structure checksum failure: 0xf0 != 0xf3

This is deterministic (same 0xf0-computed/0xf3-stored mismatch on
every probe, on both cages, across power cycles) -- a firmware/
programming defect of this generic OEM module's EEPROM contents, not
an I2C read glitch or a damaged/untrustworthy module. All other base-
structure fields (vendor name, vendor PN, vendor rev, phys id,
connector, bitrate, length) read back correctly and consistently.

sfp_sm_mod_probe() in drivers/net/phy/sfp.c already tolerates this
exact situation for Cotsworks modules (their comment: "Cotsworks do
not seem to update the checksums when they do the final programming
...") by downgrading the checksum mismatch to a warning instead of
returning -EINVAL. For any other vendor the mismatch is fatal: the SFP
state machine never leaves probe, the port never reaches
SFP_S_LINK_UP, and the netdev is permanently stuck at NO-CARRIER --
confirmed on hardware (LS1046A Mono Gateway, eth3/eth4, both cages)
where carrier_changes stayed 0 indefinitely, surviving both a warm
reboot and a full cold power-cycle, until this module's checksum
failure was made non-fatal.

Extend the existing Cotsworks leniency to this specific OEM PN,
matching the file's own precedent rather than relaxing the check
generally for all vendors.

Signed-off-by: VyOS LS1046A Build <build@vyos.io>
---
 drivers/net/phy/sfp.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
--- a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
@@ -2324,6 +2324,7 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	struct sfp_eeprom_id id;
 	bool cotsworks_sfbg;
 	unsigned int mask;
+	bool oem_h10g_cu1m;
 	bool cotsworks;
 	u8 check;
 	int ret;
@@ -2389,10 +2390,25 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 			return ret;
 	}

+	/*
+	 * OEM SFP-H10G-B-CU1M (unbranded 10G DAC) ships with a base-structure
+	 * checksum byte that never matches the computed value (observed
+	 * 0xf0 != 0xf3, deterministic across reads/modules, not an I2C
+	 * glitch) -- a firmware/programming defect of this generic module,
+	 * not an unreadable/untrustworthy EEPROM. All other fields (vendor
+	 * name/PN/rev, phys id, connector, bitrate, length) read back
+	 * correctly. Treat the same as the existing Cotsworks leniency:
+	 * warn and continue, rather than sfp_sm_mod_probe() failing the
+	 * module permanently (SFP_S_FAIL) and leaving the port at
+	 * NO-CARRIER forever.
+	 */
+	oem_h10g_cu1m = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "OEM             ", 16) &&
+			!memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFP-H10G-B-CU1M", 15);
+
 	/* Validate the checksum over the base structure */
 	check = sfp_check(&id.base, sizeof(id.base) - 1);
 	if (check != id.base.cc_base) {
-		if (cotsworks) {
+		if (cotsworks || oem_h10g_cu1m) {
 			dev_warn(sfp->dev,
 				 "EEPROM base structure checksum failure (0x%02x != 0x%02x)\n",
 				 check, id.base.cc_base);
